{"doc_id": "mb_2026_canola_insect_scouting_semantic_0002", "title": "Manitoba 2026 early-season canola insect scouting", "text": "In Manitoba canola, scout early-season fields for flea beetles, cutworms, red turnip beetles, and diamondback moth. For cutworm, look in May and June for notched, wilted, dead, or cut-off seedlings and bare patches; larvae may be close to recently damaged plants. The 2026 guide gives a nominal cutworm decision point of 25 to 30 percent stand reduction and notes that patch treatment may be more economical than whole-field treatment when injury is localized. For flea beetles, examine ten randomly selected plants at each stop and estimate leaf-area loss. The guide's 25 percent leaf-area-destruction threshold applies while the canola is in the seedling stage and flea beetles are still present. If crop stage is unknown or the crop is beyond the seedling stage, do not treat 25 percent as a context-free action threshold. Threshold evidence supports a management decision but does not select or authorize a pesticide.", "source": "https://www.gov.mb.ca/agriculture/crops/guides-and-publications/", "source_id": "mb_2026_canola_insect_scouting", "download_url": "https://www.gov.mb.ca/agriculture/crops/guides-and-publications/pubs/guide-crop-protection-insects-2026.pdf", "publisher": "Manitoba Agriculture", "license": "redistributable", "license_snapshot": {"status": "redistributable", "identifier": "OpenMB Information and Data Use Licence", "evidence_url": "https://www.gov.mb.ca/legal/copyright.html", "reviewed_on": "2026-07-24", "attribution": "Contains information from the Manitoba government, licensed under the OpenMB Information and Data Use Licence (Manitoba.ca/OpenMB).", "permits_modification": true, "permits_commercial": true, "permits_redistribution": true}, "sft_status": "unknown", "source_type": "applied_guidance", "document_type": "current_provincial_crop_scouting_guide", "format": "pdf", "region": ["Manitoba"], "jurisdiction": ["Manitoba"]