{"doc_id": "mb_2026_canola_insect_scouting_semantic_0003", "title": "Manitoba 2026 canola sweep-net and area sampling for lygus bugs and diamondback moth", "text": "After canola flowering, sample lygus bugs with ten sweeps of a 38-centimetre net at each of at least five sites; if counts are concerning, the 2026 Manitoba guide calls for at least 15 samples before judging economics. Counts are most comparable on a sunny day above 20 degrees Celsius with a dry canopy. A range of 20 to 30 lygus bugs per ten sweeps is the guide's threshold under good conditions, with the lower end more relevant to stressed crops. For diamondback moth, shake plants from a 50 by 50 centimetre area onto a light surface, multiply the larval count by four for larvae per square metre, and repeat in at least five areas. The nominal range depends on crop stage and stand density, so record those factors instead of treating a count as context-free.", "source": "https://www.gov.mb.ca/agriculture/crops/guides-and-publications/", "source_id": "mb_2026_canola_insect_scouting", "download_url": "https://www.gov.mb.ca/agriculture/crops/guides-and-publications/pubs/guide-crop-protection-insects-2026.pdf", "publisher": "Manitoba Agriculture", "license": "redistributable", "license_snapshot": {"status": "redistributable", "identifier": "OpenMB Information and Data Use Licence", "evidence_url": "https://www.gov.mb.ca/legal/copyright.html", "reviewed_on": "2026-07-24", "attribution": "Contains information from the Manitoba government, licensed under the OpenMB Information and Data Use Licence (Manitoba.ca/OpenMB).", "permits_modification": true, "permits_commercial": true, "permits_redistribution": true}, "sft_status": "unknown", "source_type": "applied_guidance", "document_type": "current_provincial_crop_scouting_guide", "format": "pdf", "region": ["Manitoba"], "jurisdiction": ["Manitoba"], "language": ["en-CA"], "currency": {"status": "current_2026_edition", "review_interval_days": 90, "volatile": true}, "r